<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rígsþula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rígsmál, The Lay of Ríg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rígsþula (The Lay of Ríg) is an Eddic poem, preserved incomplete in the 14th-century Codex Wormianus, in which a god named Ríg fathers the social classes of mankind. Visiting three couples in turn, he fathers Þræll, ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, Karl, ancestor of free farmers and craftsmen, and Jarl, ancestor of the warrior nobility; Jarl's youngest son Konr ungr, skilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runes">runes</a> and magic, stands for the origin of the word konungr, king. The prose introduction identifies Ríg with Heimdall, though some scholars think the role fits Odin better. Datings of the poem range from the 10th to the 13th century, and it breaks off as a crow urges Konr to seek the halls of Dan and Danþír.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rigs-ula.jpg" alt="Rígsþula" width="330" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.G. Collingwood (1854 - 1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rig_in_Great-grandfather's_Cottage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Ríg first comes to a farm-hut owned by Ái (great-grandfather) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edda">Edda</a> (great-grandmother), who give him poor, rough food; he sleeps between them, and nine months later Edda bears a dark son, Þræll. He marries Thír, and their 12 sons and nine daughters, with names suggesting ugliness, become the race of serfs. At a pleasant house Ríg is fed well by Afi and Amma, and their son Karl, ruddy of complexion, marries Snör; his children become the free farmers, craftsmen and herdsmen.</p><p>In a mansion Faðir and Móðir serve Ríg excellent food, and Móðir bears the fair-haired Jarl. When Jarl grows up and handles weapons, hawks, hounds and horses, Ríg returns, claims him, gives him his name, teaches him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/runes">runes</a> and urges him to seek lordship. Through warfare Jarl becomes lord of 18 homesteads and marries Erna, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hersir">Hersir</a>. Their youngest son, Konr, understands the speech of birds, quenches fire, heals minds and has the strength of eight men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The poem survives on the last surviving sheet of the Codex Wormianus (AM 242 fol), after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s Prose Edda. The name Rígr appears to come from Old Irish rí, ríg, king, cognate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rex. Finnur Jónsson and Rudolf Simek suggested the tradition transferred the name from Óðinn to Heimdall, and the prose introduction may have been added by the compiler to match the opening of Völuspá. The modern consensus ascribes the poem to an Icelandic author, though Jean Young and Ursula Dronke, among others, have argued for a Celtic origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Historians read the poem allegorically: slaves, freemen and aristocrats did not necessarily look different. Its picture of Þræll follows slave tropes of Old Norse literature, while Karl is taken to stand for the free-born peasant proprietor, the bondi. Thomas Hill held that the poem reflects an aristocratic rather than royalist view, since the king is simply the youngest son of Jarl. Georges Dumézil read Konr's magic as a Germanic adaptation of the Indo-European priestly function. Arngrímur Jónsson's epitome of the lost Skjöldungasaga and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ynglinga-saga">Ynglinga saga</a> in Snorri's Heimskringla both tie a Ríg to Danp and to Dan, from whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> were named.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rígsþula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Eddic poem <b>Rígsþula</b> tells how the god Ríg fathered Thrall, Karl and Jarl, the ancestors of slaves, free farmers and nobles (Viking Age Scandinavia).</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eddic poem <b>Rígsþula</b>, of uncertain date and preserved in a 14th-century manuscript, tells how the god Ríg fathered three sons, Thrall, Karl and Jarl, the ancestors of slaves, free farmers and nobles, and archaeology confirms a society divided along broadly those lines.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
